DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  aaiioo__ccaanncceell()  function attempts to cancel outstanding asynchronous
       I/O requests for the file descriptor _f_d.  If _a_i_o_c_b_p is NULL,  all  such
       requests  are  canceled.   Otherwise, only the request described by the
       control block pointed to by _a_i_o_c_b_p is canceled.  (See aaiioo(7) for a  de‐
       scription of the _a_i_o_c_b structure.)

       Normal  asynchronous  notification  occurs  for  canceled requests (see
       aaiioo(7) and ssiiggeevveenntt(3type)).  The request return status (aaiioo__rreettuurrnn(3))
       is set to -1, and the request error status  (aaiioo__eerrrroorr(3))  is  set  to
       EECCAANNCCEELLEEDD.   The  control  block of requests that cannot be canceled is
       not changed.

       If the request could not be canceled, then it  will  terminate  in  the
       usual  way  after performing the I/O operation.  (In this case, aaiioo__eerr‐‐
       rroorr(3) will return the status EEIINNPPRROOGGRREESSSSSS.)

       If _a_i_o_c_b_p is not NULL, and _f_d differs from  the  file  descriptor  with
       which the asynchronous operation was initiated, unspecified results oc‐
       cur.

       Which operations are cancelable is implementation‐defined.

